-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 0482 - SEA COURSES AND A FASTER LISBON READ  (dark: the owner's switch sea_courses_enabled, false; lit, the server holds a course for
--        every pair of harbours, so a standing job sails anywhere and a board card's trip goes in one press with no chart; the Lisbon read
--        is cheaper whatever the switch, with the same answers)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- THE DESIGN: docs/design/hq-command-2026-11-23.md section 2, v2 ("all the work done in HQ"). docs/dev-log/2026-11-23-sea-courses.md.
--
-- WHY: the owner, 2026-11-23: "i want this game to be strategical game, where all the work(activities) are done in HQ ... choosing best
--   options available, finding best options." The server plots no course: cmd.do_sail refuses a SAIL with no course when the straight
--   line crosses land (E_NO_COURSE). So 0481's standing `best` jobs sail only a course already sailed (a merchant lane, a coast course,
--   her own past voyage): prod's harness (before-0481, before-0482) found 0 of 8 paying Lisbon sales with such a course ("coursed": 0).
--   And an HQ card's trip needed the browser's chart to plot it. Second: the board read takes ~3.1 s on prod, ~2.6 s of it
--   world.best_sales at Lisbon (cut by its own 2.5 s budget).
--
-- WHAT (DECIDED BY DEFAULT; the owner may reverse any of it):
--   1. THE STORED COURSES public.sea_courses: one row a harbour, holding the course from it to every harbour after it by code ("C" order),
--      as the browser's own course author plots it (src/domain/passage proposeCourse: src/lib/sea findPath over the served raster, then
--      refineCourse off the coast mask), roadstead to roadstead, every point to 4 decimals (about 11 m). Written offline by
--      scripts/build-sea-courses.mjs on this very world (the seed's digest of the harbours, their roadsteads and the raster is checked
--      below; prod's equals it, before-0482). Each course is held to the drawn land by the no-land audit's own reading (none crosses), and
--      decoded and judged by voyage.path_refusal, cmd.do_sail's own door, BOTH ways: a pair stored once is sailed either way. Packed: the
--      first point and then each point's difference, as zigzag base-128 varints, one bytea a harbour with the byte offset of each course.
--      public.sea_course(from, to) decodes one (reversed when asked the other way); null when none (the same harbour, a non-harbour, or one
--      of the three pairs whose two harbours share one roadstead).
--   2. THE COURSE A JOB SAILS (public.fleet_job_course, by a hunk): lit, after a merchant lane, a coast course and her own voyage, the
--      stored course. So a `best` job sails her best sale wherever it is, a fixed route needs no voyage sailed first, and cmd.set_fleet_job
--      refuses E_NO_COURSE only where no water joins two harbours. cmd.do_sail still judges every course at every departure.
--   3. ONE WRITER OF A SALE'S LINES, public.sale_lines(fleet, from, sale, stops): BUY <good> <units> (none for her cargo), SAIL through the
--      stops (each with its course), SELL <good> ALL. 0481's `best` branch wrote these; it is moved here whole (public.fleet_job_plan, by a
--      hunk), and lit, each HQ trade card's act carries them as `lines` (public.hq_board_for, by a hunk): the press sends them as they
--      are (servedPress.ts), so a card goes in one press without the chart. Dark, the act is 0480's exactly (the client plots).
--   4. THE LISBON READ, CHEAPER, SAME ANSWERS (whatever the switch). Measured (the dev-log): world.best_sales at Lisbon spent ~16 % in ONE
--      voyage.reach_passage, ~80 % of that in building the harbours' distance matrix out of 254 jsonb documents (64,516 cells) every call;
--      and every good re-read her hulls and her speed's parts once a good. Now:
--        a. public.sea_reach_matrix holds that very float8[] (and its harbours' ids and codes in the same order); voyage.reach_passage
--           (by a hunk) reads it when its harbours are the call's own, else builds it as before. It is kept by public.sea_reach_matrix_keep,
--           a statement trigger on every write to public.sea_reaches and on a harbour added, removed, renamed or re-kinded (public.ports:
--           insert, delete, truncate, update of code or kind; the game's only run-time update of ports is crew_pool). Every board, best
--           sale and job plan that asks a passage is the cheaper.
--        b. world.best_sales (by a hunk) reads world.trip_hulls and voyage.fleet_speed_parts ONCE and hands them to world.board_trips (by a
--           hunk) in the context it already passes (`trip_hulls`, `speed_parts`, `trip_fleet`); board_trips takes them only when they are
--           for its own fleet and of the right shape, else reads them as before. One fleet, one snapshot (a STABLE read): the same values.
--      NOT CACHED: anything that moves with the market, the clock or her purse. The answers are the same to the digit: the self-assert
--      and proof 185 compare every piece (the matrix, reach_passage, board_legs, board_sell and board_trips for every good Lisbon sells)
--      with and without the change; world.best_sales itself (time-budgeted) is never compared with a second call.
-- ONE AUTHORITY EACH: a stored course, public.sea_course; the course a job sails, public.fleet_job_course; a sale's lines, public.sale_lines;
--   the distance matrix, voyage.reach_passage's own SELECT (public.sea_reach_matrix_keep runs the same one).
-- NOT CHANGED: cmd.do_sail and every door (a SAIL is judged exactly as before), every command, the beat, the client's chart.
-- ROWS. public.sea_courses: 254 rows written once here (see the seed's line below for courses, points and bytes); public.sea_reach_matrix:
--   one row. Dark and lit, nothing is written at run time (the matrix is rewritten only when the harbours or their miles change: migrations).
--   -- retention: public.sea_courses is none, bounded by one row a harbour (seeded here, rewritten only by a migration that re-ships it);
--      public.sea_reach_matrix is none, bounded by one row (id = 1)
-- COST. Lit, a stored course is one row read and ~85 bytes decoded (well under 1 ms); a board card adds one public.sale_lines (a few course
--   reads). The matrix read is one row (~0.5 MB, detoasted once a call) instead of 254 documents. Measured in the dev-log.
-- LOCKS: CREATE TABLE x2 (new), CREATE FUNCTION x5, CREATE OR REPLACE x6 by hunks (public.fleet_job_course, public.fleet_job_plan,
--   public.hq_board_for, voyage.reach_passage, world.best_sales, world.board_trips), CREATE TRIGGER x2 (public.sea_reaches, public.ports:
--   SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE, brief), one knob. TIME: a few seconds. BUDGET: statement_timeout 120 s, lock_timeout 5 s; fails above 60 s.
-- ROLLBACK (forward, owner only): QUICK, sea_courses_enabled false (jobs and cards are 0481's and 0480's exactly; the faster read stays,
--   it changes no answer). Full: a new migration reversing the six hunks (OLD text below verbatim), dropping the two triggers, the five
--   functions and the two tables, deleting the knob.
-- SELF-ASSERT: the seed's world is this world; the hunks once each and reversing; no other function moved; the hunked bodies' grants,
--   volatility, definer, search path and owner unmoved; the new functions server only; the tables no client's; the knob false; the seed
--   whole (rows, courses, bytes) and its three probe courses decoded to the point, both ways; a sample of stored courses taken by the
--   door both ways; the kept matrix equal to the one built, reach_passage the same with and without it, the trigger keeping it.
--   THE PROBE (rolled back; deterministic: world.best_sales is never asked; one transaction, one now(); the scene starts at 00:00 UTC of
--   the day it runs, so it starts before 23:30 UTC and never straddles midnight): a fleet at Lisbon; her board_legs, board_trips and
--   board_sell for every good Lisbon sells, the same with the hoisted context as without; dark, no course where no lane runs and
--   set_fleet_job refuses E_NO_COURSE there; lit, the fixed job is set and the tick sends BUY ALL / SAIL (the stored course) / SELL ALL and
--   cmd.do_sail takes it: she departs. Proof 185 plays the rest (a best run, a board card's lines sent, the whole table, the cost).
-- VERSION: 0482 = 0481 + 1 (prod head 0481; this file's hunks are anchored on bodies whose md5 prod's equals, before-0482).
-- news: none (dark: nothing changes for a player until the owner lights sea_courses_enabled; the faster read changes no answer)
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

set local statement_timeout = '120s';
set local lock_timeout = '5s';

